  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Who can say yes</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Holding the records is not the same as holding the right to license them. Two questions need a clear answer before a buyer sees anything: does the company have the rights, and does the person approving have the authority to approve.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Records your staff created</strong>Work produced by employees as part of their jobs is generally owned by the employer as a work made for hire, unless an agreement says otherwise.</li>
        <li><strong>Contractors and agencies</strong>Their material usually belongs to whoever wrote the assignment, so it needs its own written assignment before it can be included.</li>
        <li><strong>Customer and client material</strong>Customer contracts and privacy notices can limit what may be licensed, or require consent first.</li>
        <li><strong>Content inside your tools</strong>Third-party and open-source material can carry its own terms, even when it sits in your own systems.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">The buyers work through these before they price anything. Where a category stays unclear, it is normally left out of scope rather than guessed at. None of this is legal advice, and your own counsel can review the position for your company.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

